The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
for (i = 0; i < count; i++) {
    skb_put_u8(dev->recv_skb, *data++);
    if (!pn532_uart_rx_is_frame(dev->recv_skb))
        continue;
    // reset only when frame is complete
    pn533_recv_frame(dev->priv, dev->recv_skb, 0);
    dev->recv_skb = alloc_skb(PN532_UART_SKB_BUFF_LEN, GFP_KERNEL);
}
```
Reason:
The loop appends each incoming byte to `dev->recv_skb` unconditionally. The buffer is only reset (freed and reallocated) when a complete PN532 frame is recognized. If a continuous stream of malformed bytes never forms a valid frame, the skb grows without bound until `skb_put_u8()` exceeds the allocated tailroom, causing a memory exhaustion or overflow.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
for (i = 0; i < count; i++) {
    if (unlikely(!skb_tailroom(dev->recv_skb)))
        skb_trim(dev->recv_skb, 0);

    skb_put_u8(dev->recv_skb, *data++);
    if (!pn532_uart_rx_is_frame(dev->recv_skb))
        continue;

    pn533_recv_frame(dev->priv, dev->recv_skb, 0);
    dev->recv_skb = alloc_skb(PN532_UART_SKB_BUFF_LEN, GFP_KERNEL);
}
```
Reason:
Before appending a new byte, `skb_tailroom()` checks if the skb has room. If not, `skb_trim()` discards the entire accumulated partial frame (sets data length to 0). This prevents the skb from growing past its allocated size, limiting memory usage to a single `PN532_UART_SKB_BUFF_LEN` buffer even when receiving malformed data.

